NAME
     kvlat - a key-value service, and an open-loop load to measure it with

SYNOPSIS
     kvlat serve [-p PORT]
     kvlat load -c HOST[,HOST2] [-p PORT] [-r RATE] [-t SECS] [-b MS]
                [-m MARK] [-k MS]

DESCRIPTION
     kvlat measures what a service sees while the machine it runs on
     is revised or moved. It builds for this system and for Linux.

     serve answers one request per line on TCP port PORT (6379): "GET
     key" with "VALUE value" or "NONE", and "SET key value" with "OK".
     It takes any number of connections, in one thread.

     load sends RATE requests a second (1000), one SET for every ten
     GETs, for SECS seconds (10), on one connection. Each request is
     sent at its scheduled time, whatever has been answered, so a
     stall shows as latency and not as fewer requests. A request's
     latency runs from its scheduled time to its answer, however late.
     For every bin of MS milliseconds (100) it prints the requests,
     the 50th and 99th percentiles and the maximum, in microseconds,
     and the errors. At the end it prints the totals and the gap, the
     longest time without an answer.

     A request is an error if the connection it was sent on was
     closed, or if it is unanswered 5 s after the run ends.

OPTIONS
     -c HOST[,HOST2]  the service; with two addresses, a connection
                      made again goes to the other one
     -m MARK          print MARK and the time when "mark" arrives on
                      standard input, to place an event in the bins
     -k MS            give a connection up when requests have waited
                      MS milliseconds with no answer, count what was
                      queued on it as errors, and make it again

EXAMPLES
     kvlat serve &
     kvlat load -c 10.7.0.50 -r 500 -t 40 -b 100 -k 2000

SEE ALSO
     netbench(1), nc(1)
